        "title": "Repository Troubleshooting and Operations",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 8, section 11: Repository Troubleshooting and Operations. Explain how to diagnose repository connection failures, DNS and TLS problems, authentication errors, missing versions, stale index metadata, and inaccessible packages. Describe a layered troubleshooting process that isolates client, network, authorization, and publisher-side failures.\n\nTechnical workflow and implementation: Start by inspecting the chart directory, the installed Helm version, and the relevant chart metadata. Identify the input files, repository configuration, dependency declarations, and values that control the operation. Use the appropriate Helm commands to validate the configuration and inspect the result. For chart authoring tasks, run helm lint and helm template with the intended values files. For repository or dependency tasks, verify the source, resolved version, downloaded artifact, and package contents. Do not assume that a successful command proves the resulting chart is suitable for production.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Create a small test chart or use an approved sample chart in a non-production workspace. Apply the configuration described in this section, execute the relevant Helm workflow, and capture the command output. Inspect the resulting metadata, dependency package, or rendered Kubernetes manifests. Repeat the procedure from a clean workspace or CI runner to confirm that the result does not depend on undocumented local settings. Record the exact chart version and configuration used during the exercise.\n\nProduction engineering considerations: Explain how to diagnose repository connection failures, DNS and TLS problems, authentication errors, missing versions, stale index metadata, and inaccessible packages. Describe a layered troubleshooting process that isolates client, network, authorization, and publisher-side failures. Apply peer review to changes affecting chart sources, dependency versions, repository configuration, credentials, and release values. Keep secrets out of Chart.yaml, values files, scripts, and source control. Use least-privilege identities for repository access and separate chart retrieval permissions from cluster deployment permissions. Pin versions when repeatability matters, and inspect changes before promoting a chart artifact.\n\nValidation and troubleshooting: When the operation fails, classify the failure before changing configuration. Check chart metadata and syntax, repository URL and DNS resolution, TLS trust, authentication, version availability, lock file consistency, package retrieval, and rendered output in a deliberate order. Preserve error messages and pipeline logs. Change one variable at a time, repeat the validation, and document the confirmed root cause rather than hiding the failure with an unrelated workaround.\n\nRelease readiness: Before production use, verify the chart name, chart version, source repository or registry, dependency versions, values supplied, and generated resource changes. For Kubernetes resources, review namespaces, labels, selectors, service accounts, security contexts, resource requests and limits, storage settings, and exposed service endpoints where relevant. Test in a controlled namespace, retain the exact artifact that passed validation, and promote that reviewed artifact through the release process."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Release Installation",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-9-section-01",
        "title": "Helm Release Installation Fundamentals",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 9, section 1: Helm Release Installation Fundamentals. Explain the meaning of a Helm release and how helm install creates a named deployment instance from a chart. Cover the relationship between chart package, release name, namespace, supplied values, rendered templates, and Kubernetes resources. Distinguish installing a chart from simply downloading or rendering it.\n\nTechnical explanation: Helm manages a release by combining chart metadata, templates, values, and Kubernetes API operations. Engineers must understand which inputs determine the generated manifests and which cluster resources are affected. Inspect the chart version, release name, namespace, supplied values, and current release state before changing anything. Separate chart-level behavior from Kubernetes controller behavior so that a Helm command result is not mistaken for proof of application health.\n\nPractical implementation: Use a dedicated test namespace and an approved sample chart to reproduce the workflow. Inspect Chart.yaml and values.yaml, run helm lint, and render the expected manifests with helm template. Execute the relevant release command only after reviewing the generated resources. Verify the resulting state using helm status, helm history, kubectl get, kubectl describe, and application logs where appropriate. Record the exact commands, chart version, values files, and release revision.\n\nProduction DevOps practice: Explain the meaning of a Helm release and how helm install creates a named deployment instance from a chart. Cover the relationship between chart package, release name, namespace, supplied values, rendered templates, and Kubernetes resources. Distinguish installing a chart from simply downloading or rendering it. Use explicit chart versions and reviewed configuration. Protect cluster credentials and use a deployment identity with only the permissions required for the target namespace. Require peer review for changes that affect workload images, resource limits, service exposure, persistent storage, security contexts, or access permissions. Keep release artifacts and deployment records traceable to the source commit and pipeline execution.\n\nTroubleshooting approach: When a release operation fails, preserve the Helm error and inspect the release status before attempting another operation. Review Kubernetes events, pod scheduling, image pull status, container logs, readiness and liveness probes, storage attachment, and API admission errors. For upgrade problems, compare the intended manifests with the current release revision. For rollback problems, confirm the selected revision and check whether external data or application state can safely support the restored version.\n\nVerification and operational outcome: Define success using observable conditions rather than the command exit code alone. Confirm that the expected revision is active, workloads reach the required readiness state, services expose the correct endpoints, and application health checks pass. Check that resource requests, limits, security settings, and environment-specific configuration remain correct. Document the outcome and retain the exact chart package and values used so another engineer can reproduce or investigate the release."
