        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 10, section 14: Production Upgrade Strategy and Release Governance. Explain production upgrade planning using maintenance windows, canary or staged rollout strategies, compatibility checks, monitoring, change records, and rollback criteria. Cover how teams decide whether to continue, pause, or reverse a release based on measurable application health.\n\nTechnical explanation: Helm manages a release by combining chart metadata, templates, values, and Kubernetes API operations. Engineers must understand which inputs determine the generated manifests and which cluster resources are affected. Inspect the chart version, release name, namespace, supplied values, and current release state before changing anything. Separate chart-level behavior from Kubernetes controller behavior so that a Helm command result is not mistaken for proof of application health.\n\nPractical implementation: Use a dedicated test namespace and an approved sample chart to reproduce the workflow. Inspect Chart.yaml and values.yaml, run helm lint, and render the expected manifests with helm template. Execute the relevant release command only after reviewing the generated resources. Verify the resulting state using helm status, helm history, kubectl get, kubectl describe, and application logs where appropriate. Record the exact commands, chart version, values files, and release revision.\n\nProduction DevOps practice: Explain production upgrade planning using maintenance windows, canary or staged rollout strategies, compatibility checks, monitoring, change records, and rollback criteria. Cover how teams decide whether to continue, pause, or reverse a release based on measurable application health. Use explicit chart versions and reviewed configuration. Protect cluster credentials and use a deployment identity with only the permissions required for the target namespace. Require peer review for changes that affect workload images, resource limits, service exposure, persistent storage, security contexts, or access permissions. Keep release artifacts and deployment records traceable to the source commit and pipeline execution.\n\nTroubleshooting approach: When a release operation fails, preserve the Helm error and inspect the release status before attempting another operation. Review Kubernetes events, pod scheduling, image pull status, container logs, readiness and liveness probes, storage attachment, and API admission errors. For upgrade problems, compare the intended manifests with the current release revision. For rollback problems, confirm the selected revision and check whether external data or application state can safely support the restored version.\n\nVerification and operational outcome: Define success using observable conditions rather than the command exit code alone. Confirm that the expected revision is active, workloads reach the required readiness state, services expose the correct endpoints, and application health checks pass. Check that resource requests, limits, security settings, and environment-specific configuration remain correct. Document the outcome and retain the exact chart package and values used so another engineer can reproduce or investigate the release."
