        "title": "Sync Operation Phases and Results",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 17, section 6: Sync Operation Phases and Results. Explain the lifecycle of a synchronization operation, including operation initiation, resource application, hooks where configured, health assessment, and final operation result. Cover successful, failed, running, and terminated operations. Explain how an Application can have a successful sync operation but later become unhealthy because a workload fails after the resources were accepted by Kubernetes.\n\nTechnical workflow and evidence: Identify the Argo CD Application, source repository, target revision, destination cluster, namespace, and Kubernetes resources involved. Inspect the Application status and resource tree, then compare the desired manifests with the live objects. Capture the relevant resource diff, conditions, operation information, Kubernetes events, and component logs. Establish whether the observed state is caused by a source change, controller behavior, external modification, or workload failure.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Use a disposable test namespace and a non-production Application. Record the initial desired and live configuration, introduce one controlled change, and observe the resulting status and resource diff. Test the relevant recovery or reconciliation behavior only after confirming resource ownership and safety. Verify the final resource specification, readiness, and application behavior, and retain evidence of the change and its resolution.\n\nProduction engineering and safety: Explain the lifecycle of a synchronization operation, including operation initiation, resource application, hooks where configured, health assessment, and final operation result. Cover successful, failed, running, and terminated operations. Explain how an Application can have a successful sync operation but later become unhealthy because a workload fails after the resources were accepted by Kubernetes. Apply controlled access to live cluster resources and make Git changes through reviewed pull requests. Use narrowly scoped exceptions when fields are intentionally managed by another controller. Before enabling automated correction or pruning, evaluate the impact on stateful workloads, shared resources, and external integrations. Preserve an emergency-change process that records the action and restores agreement between the live system and the approved desired state.\n\nTroubleshooting and validation: Determine whether the issue originates in desired manifests, live object changes, rendering differences, API defaulting, external controllers, resource health, or reconciliation policy. Inspect the exact affected fields and managed ownership before modifying configuration. Validate any proposed correction through manifest rendering and review, then confirm that the comparison result changes as expected and that Kubernetes resources reach the intended operational condition.\n\nOperational readiness: Verify the selected source revision, destination, resource ownership, comparison result, reconciliation outcome, and application health. Confirm that no unrelated resources were changed or removed. Review alerts, audit records, Git history, and relevant logs. Document the root cause, corrective action, approved exceptions, and prevention measures so future operators can distinguish expected differences from a genuine production configuration problem."
