        "id": "stage-11-topic-20-section-01",
        "title": "Argo CD Troubleshooting Methodology",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 20, section 1: Argo CD Troubleshooting Methodology. Explain a structured troubleshooting process for Argo CD incidents. Begin with the affected Application, expected behavior, actual behavior, time of failure, and recent changes. Classify the failure into identity, repository connectivity, manifest generation, comparison, synchronization, Kubernetes API access, or workload health. Explain why preserving evidence and isolating the failing layer prevents unnecessary restarts and risky configuration changes.\n\nTechnical investigation and implementation: Identify the affected Application, repository revision, source path, destination cluster, namespace, resource, and responsible Argo CD component. Capture the exact error message and relevant timestamps. Inspect Application conditions, operation details, generated manifests, Kubernetes events, and component logs. Reproduce the issue in a controlled environment when possible and avoid making unverified changes to production resources.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Reproduce the scenario using a non-production application and disposable namespace. Introduce one controlled fault related to the topic, observe the reported symptoms, and collect evidence from Argo CD and Kubernetes. Identify the responsible configuration or component, apply a minimal corrective change through the approved workflow, and verify that synchronization and workload health return to the expected state.\n\nProduction engineering and security: Explain a structured troubleshooting process for Argo CD incidents. Begin with the affected Application, expected behavior, actual behavior, time of failure, and recent changes. Classify the failure into identity, repository connectivity, manifest generation, comparison, synchronization, Kubernetes API access, or workload health. Explain why preserving evidence and isolating the failing layer prevents unnecessary restarts and risky configuration changes. Preserve least-privilege access, protect repository and cluster credentials, and follow change approval requirements for production modifications. Avoid exposing tokens, private keys, passwords, or sensitive manifest data in logs and incident reports. Before performing deletion, rollback, credential rotation, or permission changes, understand the potential impact on dependent applications and stateful resources.\n\nRoot-cause validation and recovery: Separate the immediate symptom from the underlying cause. Confirm whether the failure is related to identity, source access, rendering, comparison, policy, destination authorization, resource admission, scheduling, or application health. Correct the authoritative configuration and verify the resulting Git revision, generated manifests, sync operation, resource state, and application health. Do not rely solely on a green status indicator when critical workloads remain unhealthy.\n\nOperational evidence and prevention: Record the incident timeline, affected resources, relevant logs, repository revision, corrective change, approval, and post-recovery checks. Identify whether monitoring, validation, documentation, access controls, or deployment safeguards could prevent recurrence. Update the runbook with verified diagnostic commands and recovery steps so the next engineer can investigate the same failure consistently and safely."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-11-topic-20-section-02",
        "title": "Application Stuck in OutOfSync State",
        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 20, section 2: Application Stuck in OutOfSync State. Explain why an Application may remain OutOfSync even after a deployment appears successful. Cover differences between desired and live manifests, unmanaged field changes, defaulted Kubernetes fields, ignored differences, source revision selection, and resource tracking. Explain how to inspect the resource-level diff and determine whether the mismatch is expected, harmless, or evidence of a real deployment problem.\n\nTechnical investigation and implementation: Identify the affected Application, repository revision, source path, destination cluster, namespace, resource, and responsible Argo CD component. Capture the exact error message and relevant timestamps. Inspect Application conditions, operation details, generated manifests, Kubernetes events, and component logs. Reproduce the issue in a controlled environment when possible and avoid making unverified changes to production resources.\n\nPractical DevOps exercise: Reproduce the scenario using a non-production application and disposable namespace. Introduce one controlled fault related to the topic, observe the reported symptoms, and collect evidence from Argo CD and Kubernetes. Identify the responsible configuration or component, apply a minimal corrective change through the approved workflow, and verify that synchronization and workload health return to the expected state.\n\nProduction engineering and security: Explain why an Application may remain OutOfSync even after a deployment appears successful. Cover differences between desired and live manifests, unmanaged field changes, defaulted Kubernetes fields, ignored differences, source revision selection, and resource tracking. Explain how to inspect the resource-level diff and determine whether the mismatch is expected, harmless, or evidence of a real deployment problem. Preserve least-privilege access, protect repository and cluster credentials, and follow change approval requirements for production modifications. Avoid exposing tokens, private keys, passwords, or sensitive manifest data in logs and incident reports. Before performing deletion, rollback, credential rotation, or permission changes, understand the potential impact on dependent applications and stateful resources.\n\nRoot-cause validation and recovery: Separate the immediate symptom from the underlying cause. Confirm whether the failure is related to identity, source access, rendering, comparison, policy, destination authorization, resource admission, scheduling, or application health. Correct the authoritative configuration and verify the resulting Git revision, generated manifests, sync operation, resource state, and application health. Do not rely solely on a green status indicator when critical workloads remain unhealthy.\n\nOperational evidence and prevention: Record the incident timeline, affected resources, relevant logs, repository revision, corrective change, approval, and post-recovery checks. Identify whether monitoring, validation, documentation, access controls, or deployment safeguards could prevent recurrence. Update the runbook with verified diagnostic commands and recovery steps so the next engineer can investigate the same failure consistently and safely."
