        "content": "Stage 11 navigation 21, section 10: Production AppProject and Cluster Permissions. Create or select an AppProject that restricts approved source repositories, destination clusters, namespaces, and Kubernetes resource kinds. Configure the required destination cluster registration and least-privilege access. Explain how project boundaries prevent an application from deploying into an unintended namespace or cluster. Validate the Application against project restrictions before starting synchronization, and retain evidence of the permissions required by the deployment.\n\nImplementation workflow: Identify the repository, chart version, image artifact, production values, Argo CD Application, AppProject, target cluster, and namespace involved in this exercise. Work from version-controlled configuration and inspect the generated manifests before applying changes. Use the relevant Helm commands, Argo CD interfaces, Kubernetes inspection commands, and component logs to verify each step. Keep the deployment process repeatable and record the exact revision and configuration used.\n\nHands-on execution: Perform the task first in a disposable or non-production environment. Record the expected state, introduce one controlled configuration or operational change, and observe the actual result. Inspect the generated resources and application status, then compare them with the declared desired state. Where the topic involves recovery, perform a controlled rollback or corrective change and confirm that the application returns to the documented baseline.\n\nProduction engineering considerations: Create or select an AppProject that restricts approved source repositories, destination clusters, namespaces, and Kubernetes resource kinds. Configure the required destination cluster registration and least-privilege access. Explain how project boundaries prevent an application from deploying into an unintended namespace or cluster. Validate the Application against project restrictions before starting synchronization, and retain evidence of the permissions required by the deployment. Apply peer review and change approval to high-impact changes. Use least-privilege identities, protect secrets, verify destination boundaries, and assess the effect of resource deletion or workload replacement. Preserve service availability through suitable replica counts, readiness checks, resource sizing, and recovery planning. Avoid undocumented manual changes that create configuration drift or weaken the audit trail.\n\nTroubleshooting and evidence collection: If the expected result is not reached, classify the failure as a repository, authentication, rendering, policy, synchronization, Kubernetes admission, scheduling, networking, or application health problem. Capture relevant error messages, Application conditions, operation details, component logs, Kubernetes events, and workload status. Correct the underlying source or permission issue, then verify the resulting state instead of repeatedly retrying an unchanged failed operation.\n\nAcceptance criteria and operational handover: Confirm the expected chart and image versions, production configuration, destination, deployed resource inventory, synchronization outcome, workload health, security controls, and recovery path. Record the Git revision, approvals, relevant commands, validation output, and any known limitations. Provide clear instructions for monitoring, routine upgrades, credential rotation, incident response, and rollback so the environment can be operated safely by another engineer."
